The graphics stack — Linux and Windows differ here
---------------------------------------------------

On Linux these are system shared libraries: present on the machine, linked at
run time, and NOT redistributed by this project. They are Cairo (LGPL 2.1 /
MPL 1.1), FreeType (FTL / GPLv2 dual licence, used under the FTL), fontconfig
(MIT-style) and libX11 (MIT). There is no JACK dependency: this project ships
the VST3 bundle only.

On Windows there is no system copy of any of them, so the Windows release
STATICALLY LINKS and therefore redistributes them inside Rations.vst3. That changes
the licence character of the shipped binary, so they are named here in full,
with the versions scripts/build-win-deps.sh pins:

  cairo 1.18.4       https://cairographics.org/
                     LGPL 2.1-or-later OR MPL 1.1 (dual).
  pixman 0.44.0      https://cairographics.org/     MIT.
  FreeType 2.13.3    https://freetype.org/          FTL OR GPLv2 (dual).
  libpng 1.6.48      http://libpng.org/pub/png/     libpng/zlib licence.
  zlib 1.3.1         https://zlib.net/              zlib licence.

  fontconfig is NOT part of the Windows build. It is linked on Linux but never
  called — there is no Fc* call anywhere in this tree — and FreeType opens the
  bundled .ttf files by path, so the Windows build simply drops it.

  FreeType: the FTL is elected, not the GPLv2 option. The FTL requires this
  credit to appear in the documentation of any product using it, so here it is
  verbatim:

      Portions of this software are copyright (c) 2024 The FreeType Project
      (www.freetype.org).  All rights reserved.

  FreeType is built with one modification, made by scripts/build-win-deps.sh
  rather than carried as a patch: its meson.build defines DLL_EXPORT on Windows
  without testing default_library, which makes a STATIC build export the whole
  FreeType API from whatever links it. The build script removes that define. No
  FreeType source file is changed.

  cairo and the LGPL: cairo is linked statically into Rations.vst3, which
  LGPL 2.1 section 6 permits provided the recipient can relink the work against a
  modified cairo. That condition is met by publication rather than by a written
  offer: Rations' complete corresponding source is published under the MIT
  Licence, the exact cairo version and the exact configuration used to build it
  are in scripts/build-win-deps.sh in this same repository, and the whole
  plug-in rebuilds from that source with one cmake invocation. Anyone may
  substitute their own cairo and rebuild. If cairo's LGPL terms are ever
  inconvenient, its MPL 1.1 arm is available instead — no cairo source file is
  modified either way.

